Q: How to fix ElementClickInterceptedException in Selenium Java A: <h2><a href="https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/docs/api/java/org/openqa/selenium/ElementClickInterceptedException.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ElementClickInterceptedException</a></h2>
<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/62262557/7429447">ElementClickInterceptedException</a> occurs when Selenium tries to click on a webelement while another element is blocking or covering it at that moment which inturn means Selenium found the element but could not invoke click on it because something else received the click.</p>
<hr />
<h2>This usecase</h2>
<p>Though you have used <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/scrollIntoView" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>scrollIntoView({block: 'center'})</code></a> which should scroll the element to align it vertically at the center of the scrollable container positioning it in the middle of the visible area still you should allow some sometime for the desired element to turn interactable/clickable so the CSS animation/temporarily overlays are over/removed before Selenium attempts to invoke the click on the desired element.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Solution</h2>
<p>The solution would be, once you invoke <code>scrollToElements(WebElement element)</code> as:</p>
<pre><code>public static void scrollToElements(WebElement element) {
    String jsScript = &quot;arguments[0].scrollIntoView({block: 'center'});&quot;;
    JavascriptExecutor js = (JavascriptExecutor) driver;
    js.executeScript(jsScript, element);

}
</code></pre>
<p>Induce <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/48990165/7429447">WebDriverWait</a> for the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/63301869/7429447"><em>elementToBeClickable()</em></a> as follows:</p>
<pre><code>import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.ExpectedConditions;
import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait;

new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10)).until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(element)).click();
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>References</h2>
<p>You can find a relevant detailed discussion on <em>ElementClickInterceptedException</em> in:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79947634/7429447">How to reliably wait for dynamic elements loaded via JavaScript (AJAX)?</a></li>
</ul>

Q: How to speed up bulk insertion of pairs into a std::map? A: <p>A simple way to speed up bulk insertion into a std::map is to sort the input by key and use hinted insertion.</p>
<p>When the keys are sorted, each insertion happens near the previous one, so the tree performs far fewer rotations and rebalancing operations. This takes advantage of the fact that insert(hint, value) can run in amortized constant time when the hint is correct.<br />
A simple way to speed up bulk insertion into a map is ;</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Sort the input by key</p>
</li>
<li><p>Use hinted insertion so the map can insert each element after the previous one</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>This reduces the number of tree rotations and rebalancing operations.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code>// generate random pairs
std::vector&lt;std::pair&lt;size_t, size_t&gt;&gt; vins(100000);
    for (auto&amp; p : vins) {
    p.first = rand() % 1000000;
    p.second = rand() % 2;
}


// sort by key
std::sort(vins.begin(), vins.end(),
[](auto&amp; a, auto&amp; b){ return a.first &lt; b.first; });

// hinted insertion
std::map&lt;size_t, size_t&gt; m;
auto hint = m.begin();
for (auto&amp; p : vins) {
    hint = m.insert(hint, p);
}
</code></pre>
